"and explorer.\\n2. Networks are told apart by genesis hash, not by address encoding. Chain identifiers (CAIP-2 and\\n   CAIP-10) derive from it, and every signed extrinsic commits to it.\\n3. An off-chain statement that authorizes value, such as a genesis claim statement, must bind the\\n   network (genesis hash or chain id) itself, since an address no longer encodes it.\\n4. The metadata hash commits to the prefix, so a single release runtime serves testnet and mainnet\\n   with the same prefix.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a76.5 R6.5a; \u00a79.3 R9.8.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** Registry state: the GitHub repository `paritytech/ss58-registry` reports\\n`archived: true`; its last registration request (#246, prefixes 59 and 60) was never merged.\\nEcosystem guidance: [Unifying Polkadot ecosystem address\\nformat](https://forum.polkadot.network/t/unifying-polkadot-ecosystem-address-format/10042).\\n\\n## D-0514 \u2014 Era-T conversion rules and a fail-closed miner adjustment\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-29. No earlier decision set these rules. They were a\\ndraft in ERA_T_TO_ERA_0 and a placeholder in E.38.\\n\\n**Context.** \u00a78 specified `work-vesting` only; the path that turns Era-T activity into Era-0 airdrop\\ngrants had no normative section. D-0421, D-0438 and D-0440 fixed that genesis supply goes to\\nthree airdrop cohorts and sized each pool. They did not say what earns an allocation, how the\\nsnapshot is fixed, or what the miner performance adjustment does when its inputs are missing.\\n`pallet_airdrop_vesting` applies the plain linear curve when a miner has no mainnet score. That\\nmakes missing evidence the most favourable outcome on a money path.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. Conversion distributes only the existing `genesis_supply`, with no incremental emission and no\\n   allocation funded twice. Each cohort draws only from its own airdrop pool, and cohorts do not\\n   cross-subsidize. `genesis_reserve` is not an Era-T airdrop pool.\\n2. A faucet grant, sponsored bond or transferred balance creates no allocation right by itself.\\n   Held Era-T balance and stake size are not scoring terms. Agents are scored on settled\\n   compute-channel spend and miners on PoUI-verified effective-FLOP served. Completed job count and\\n   active days are not scoring terms. A fraud-flagged account receives zero allocation, with no\\n   partial credit, unless the flag is overturned before the genesis specification is locked.\\n3. Conversion reads Era-T activity at a governance-ratified, finalized snapshot height. The\\n   per-account allocation and its hash are published before the genesis specification is locked;\\n   until then the snapshot may be revoked or corrected. The snapshot records each account's cohort,\\n   activity, fraud flag and cluster identity, and the published allocation binds the inputs used to\\n   filter and score it. The finalized Era-T chain state, the allocation file, the\\n   conversion-parameters file and their hashes are the evidence of record.\\n4. The miner performance adjustment fails closed. While a Miner grant lacks either performance\\n   input, or its testnet baseline is zero, the adjustment factor is taken as zero, so the grant\\n   unlocks nothing before its end block. The end-block rule is unchanged: every grant is fully vested\\n   there.\\n\\nScore caps and aggregation, the accounting identity, the validator activity basis, appeals, the\\nagent release horizon and the remainder disposition stay open in E.38.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a78.2 R8.3\u2013R8.5, R8.7; Appendix H.4.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** Balance carries no scoring weight because the conversion rewards work, not\\nholdings (ECON-007).\\nItem 2 replaces the E.38 placeholder, which listed completed jobs and active days among the activity\\ninputs. The pallet change for item 4 is #1175 (BUILD step 4). The conversion allocator in\\n`ai-agent-simulator` still weights completed jobs and active days (#1174).\\n\\n## D-0515 \u2014 Airdrop grant landing, claims and locked compute spend\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-29. Extends [D-0514](#d-0514--era-t-conversion-rules-and-a-fail-closed-miner-adjustment)\\nto the two \u00a78.2 requirements it left out; supersedes nothing.\\n\\n**Context.** \u00a78.2 made R8.6 (grant landing) and R8.8 (claims and locked spend) normative, but\\nD-0514 ratified only R8.3\u2013R8.5 and R8.7. Both are money-path rules, and no earlier decision set\\nthem: the landing's funding was open in #1175, and the locked-spend sinks were a draft in\\nLOCKED_COMPUTE_SPEND.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. Each Era-0 airdrop allocation is funded exactly once, as a genesis balance frozen by a\\n   `pallet_airdrop_vesting` grant whose tier matches the allocation's cohort. Creating the grant\\n   does not mint the allocation again. Which bridge writes the grants over the funded balances is\\n   left open.\\n2. `claim_vested` computes the unlocked amount at the finalized head, subtracts what was already\\n   claimed, and releases only a positive remainder from the freeze.\\n3. Still-locked principal may be spent only through `agent_transfer`, `open_channel`,\\n   `top_up_escrow` and `force_open`, for compute. That spend consumes vesting principal and never\\n   becomes freely transferable credit.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a78.2 R8.6, R8.8; Appendix H.4.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** Item 1 is the landing half of #1175 (BUILD step 1); the bridge's shape\\n(a genesis config that freezes pre-funded balances, or a root call that adopts one) is that\\nissue's open design choice. Item 2 is how the pallet already computes claims. Item 3 adopts the\\nsinks of LOCKED_COMPUTE_SPEND. Raised in review of\\n\u00a78.2 (#1952).\\n\\n## D-0517 \u2014 Airdrop unlock: miner earn-to-unlock, validator halving release\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-30, in review of #2092 and #2097. Supersedes D-0514 item 4 for\\nMiner grants and the end-block rule of R8.7 for Miner and Validator grants; narrows D-0515 item 3 to\\nAgent grants.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0514 made Miner and Validator airdrop grants vest linearly over\\n`airdrop_vesting_duration_blocks`, with a performance adjustment for miners. The team's published\\nterms were different, and the owner directed the protocol to follow them. D-0515 item 3 let still-locked\\nprincipal of any tier be spent on compute. A compute channel pays `ch.miner` by free transfer and has\\nno agent-differs-from-miner guard, so a miner could spend its own locked principal on a channel that\\npays itself and recover about 0.99 FLOP of free balance per FLOP spent, under either schedule.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. A Miner grant unlocks `\u230aP/4\u230b` of its principal `P` at its start block. The rest unlocks only\\n   against unlock credit: one FLOP per FLOP the grantee receives as the channel's miner in the payout\\n   of a session settled by `settle`, `certificate_settle` or `force_settle \u2192 finalize`, counted once\\n   the settlement is finalized and the channel dispute window has passed with no upheld fraud dispute.\\n   Block rewards, forward-availability payments, refunds, penalties and dispute bonds accrue no\\n   credit. The grant\\n   has no end block; principal not yet unlocked stays frozen. The D-0514 performance adjustment no\\n   longer applies.\\n2. A Validator grant stays frozen until block 63,072,000, the first block of halving era 1. From\\n   there, Validator grants are released one per 86,400 blocks, each releasing its remaining principal in full, until all are\\n   released. No Validator grant releases until the release order is fixed (E.38). Release lifts only\\n   the vesting freeze: it never unbonds stake, and an active validator keeps `validator_min_stake`\\n   bonded.\\n3. Only Agent grants may spend still-locked principal, and only through the four compute calls of\\n   D-0515. A miner may bond its still-locked principal as its own self-stake; it stays frozen while\\n   bonded and after unbonding until it unlocks. Otherwise still-locked Miner and Validator principal\\n   cannot be spent, transferred or used for compute; slashing of bonded stake applies to it. A slash of\\n   a grantee's bonded stake is charged first to still-frozen grant principal (added to `S`, the grant's\\n   slashed total) and removed from the grant's freeze atomically, then to other balance.\\n4. The Agent grant is unchanged: its horizon stays open in E.38, and a linear schedule would use\\n   `airdrop_vesting_duration_blocks`.\\n\\nThis settles the miner vesting divergence of #1355 and the validator unlock conflict of #1359, which\\nD-0435 marked \\\"do not build as specified\\\": a stake could unlock while still securing the set.\\n\\n**Recorded residual.** Once its quarter is liquid, a miner can buy inference from itself through an\\nagent it controls and unlock the rest at about the 1% audit earmark, roughly 0.75% of principal. No\\nruntime common-control rule binds this (E.38, E.49).\\n\\n**Open.** The Validator release order and the disposition of Miner principal that never earns its\\nunlock stay in E.38. Whether a fraud\\nverdict upheld after the dispute window reverses unlock credit follows E.44.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendix E.38, H.4; the `airdrop_vesting_duration_blocks`\\ndescription.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** Block rewards are excluded because they would turn the unlock back into a\\nclock: 72 FLOP per block is about 6.22M FLOP a day to the miner pool, enough to unlock the 900M miner\\ntranche in about 145 days whatever the demand, and rewards also pay the synthetic-task floor. The\\nValidator window opens on the \u00a79.2 Day-730 revenue cliff, and releasing one grant per day staggers\\nthat exit. The constants stay inline in R8.7 until #1175 builds the schedule, since a params entry\\nneeds a consuming implementation.\\n\\n## D-0518 \u2014 Block rewards and the subsidy are liquid on issue\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-30, in review of #2097. Supersedes D-0408's\\nauto-compounding of validator earnings into locked stake, which D-0435 left unchanged; closes E.39.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0408 auto-compounds the validator 10% leg into locked stake. The tokenomics workbook\\nhas paid validator rewards fully liquid since 2026-08-12, and E.39 held the question open (#1356).\\nR9.2 also said every unlock was block-by-block linear, which the D-0517 airdrop unlock no longer is.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. The miner and validator legs of every block reward, and the Labs/Foundation subsidy, are liquid on\\n   issue. The protocol never auto-compounds, force-stakes, vests or locks them; bonding a liquid\\n   balance as stake is the holder's choice.\\n2. The agent leg keeps accruing in its sovereign pool under R9.12, which is not a lock; once its\\n   distribution policy ratifies (E.40), every agent-leg payout is liquid on payment. The staker leg's\\n   liquidity stays open in E.40.\\n3. R9.2 no longer claims that all unlocks are block-by-block linear; it points to R9.13 for rewards\\n   and to \u00a78.2 R8.7 for airdrop grants.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a79 table, R9.2, R9.13; Appendix D; E.39 removed; E.40; H.4.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** The stake requirement is what secures the set; locking earnings on top of it\\nadds collateral the validator did not choose to post, and the workbook's validator economics assume\\nliquid earnings. The runtime still auto-compounds the validator leg (#1356).\\n\\n## D-0519 \u2014 Service-lane close boundary, claim window and provider-only redemption\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-10-04 as A + M2. The owner replied \\\"Continue on both\\\" in\\nthe session after the separate requests to approve these lane-close rules and authorize the required\\nCodex consultation. This records the session instruction, not a GitHub review signature. Paid-storage\\nlanes remain restricted to development networks under D-0512 until E.54 is ratified and implemented;\\nthe numerical claim window remains UNRATIFIED (E.54). This documentation changes no runtime behavior;\\nthe implementation is #1836.\\n\\n**Context.** R5.4g settles retrieval with cumulative payer-signed `ServiceVoucher`s. Before this\\ndecision, G.4 said `close_service_lane` stopped new authorization and let anyone holding a valid\\nvoucher redeem it. F.7 gave each `ServiceLane` a close boundary and a claim deadline. E.54 listed\\nlane-close boundaries among the items it must ratify, without defining either boundary. Two gaps\\nblocked implementation:\\n\\n1. *A signature carries no time.* Every value a signer can put in a voucher before close (a block\\n   hash, an epoch, a sequence number) is still available afterwards, so a voucher signed after\\n   close can be indistinguishable from one signed before it. A signature is provably older than a\\n   block only if the chain recorded it, or a commitment to it, before that block. Close therefore\\n   cannot be enforced against later signatures by any check on voucher contents, and a validity\\n   field in the voucher would not help, because the signer chooses its value. R5.4j already bounds\\n   the payer's exposure by the signed cap until the claim deadline, but refers to \\\"the funded lane's\\n   close and claim rules\\\" without defining them.\\n2. *Redemption order.* R5.4g rejects stale counters, and G.4 lets anyone submit, so whoever lands a\\n   voucher first picks the order. Suppose a provider holds an unredeemed voucher for a large read at\\n   sequence 7. The payer can sign sequence 8 with one more write and no read bytes, then submit it\\n   itself. That voucher is valid and pays one setup fee, after which the provider's voucher is stale\\n   and never redeems. For about one setup fee, the payer voids earned retrieval of up to an epoch.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. **Close boundary.** A lane's close boundary is the block that includes the payer's\\n   `close_service_lane`, or the reservation's end if that comes first. Once set it never moves:\\n   neither a repeated close, a revocation, nor a provider rotation changes it. Closing stops new\\n   funding. The lane keeps its reservation-provider slot and its escrow until it is finalized.\\n2. **Claim window.** The claim deadline is the close boundary plus `W` blocks. `W` is the profile's\\n   lane claim window: an E.54 limit, nonzero and at least one paid-storage epoch, fixed when the lane\\n   is funded. E.54 constrains permitted activation, challenge and funded-request timing so that all\\n   setup entitlements become redeemable, and all funded-request deadlines affecting lane claims\\n   expire, by the fixed deadline, including sequential delays and boundary rules. A profile or\\n   admission allowing an obligation beyond it is rejected; a missing bound is never zero.\\n   Qualification and admission must establish an actual runtime dispatch opportunity after all\\n   entitlement prerequisites complete while known finalized height remains within the unchanged\\n   deadline. Maturity-time arithmetic alone does not prove this under arbitrary finality jumps;\\n   affected work stays disabled, including on development networks, until qualified. This is a\\n   redemption-validity requirement, not a guarantee of transaction inclusion.\\n   The deadline is read on the finalized prefix, as \u00a71.2 requires for economic deadlines.\\n   A closing lane accepts redemption while the finalized height is at or below the deadline, and\\n   finalizes only once it is above. When no finalized height is known, neither runs. A finality\\n   stall therefore freezes the window (\u00a713 F1); catch-up can cross the deadline at once, so `W` does\\n   not promise that many blocks after recovery. Finalization refunds exactly the lane's remaining\\n   escrow. Reservation termination never releases funds still allocated to a lane.\\n3. **What close does not do.** Settlement accepts every otherwise-valid voucher up to the deadline,\\n   whether it was signed before or after the close boundary. After close, the payer's exposure is the\\n   lane's unspent budget until the deadline, and the payee stays fixed, so only the lane's provider\\n   can gain from a post-close signature. Close is never presented as proof of when a voucher was\\n   signed. Once the close is finalized, a conforming payer or delegate stops signing for the lane,\\n   and a conforming provider stops admitting new requests on it.\\n4. **Redemption.** The lane's provider dispatches `redeem_service`, directly or through an\\n   authorized proxy. A voucher redeems only if all of these hold:\\n   - its sequence exceeds the last redeemed sequence;\\n   - each cumulative counter is at least its last redeemed value;\\n   - its amount equals the R5.4g formula at the lane's pinned tariff;\\n   - its amount is above the amount already paid and within the budget.\\n\\n   Redemption pays exactly the difference to the lane's provider. It does not require that provider\\n   to still be assigned. A provider serves only against a voucher that dominates the last one it\\n   accepted.\\n5. **Lane identity.** The chain assigns lane identifiers at funding from a per-payer nonce. A nonce\\n   is never reused, even after its lane is finalized or pruned, so a voucher for one lane never\\n   redeems against another.\\n6. **Wire format.** The `ServiceVoucher` encoding and domain pinned for the development profile do\\n   not change.\\n\\n**Where the wording lands.** R5.4g takes the redemption rule of item 4. R5.4j takes items 1\\nto 3, in place of its reference to undefined close and claim rules. The F.7 `ServiceLane` record adds\\nthe claim window, and its acceptance constraints add the fixed boundary, the finalized-prefix\\ndeadline, non-reused identifiers and assignment-independent claims. In G.4, `redeem_service` names\\nthe lane's provider (or its proxy) as origin and adds the deadline rule, and\\n`close_service_lane`/`finalize_service_lane` state items 1 and 2. E.54 adds the lane claim window to\\nthe windows it ratifies, together with the residual post-close exposure of item 3.\\n\\n**Alternatives rejected.**\\n\\n- *Checkpoint-anchored close.* After the boundary, a voucher redeems only with an inclusion proof,\\n  under a provider checkpoint posted by the boundary, of a commitment that binds the signed voucher.\\n  This is the only on-chain proof that a voucher predates close. It needs checkpoint posting and a\\n  pinned commitment format first, and it does not lower the amount at risk. A later profile version\\n  may require it for lanes funded under that profile, since every lane pins its profile.\\n- *A validity field in the voucher.* The signer chooses it, so it proves nothing about close, and it\\n  would re-pin the development encoding in three languages.\\n- *Amount-ordered redemption by any submitter.* This defeats the payer's sequence attack, but it drops\\n  R5.4g's stale-counter rejection. That rejection could not be restored later without voiding\\n  vouchers providers already hold.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** No earlier decision. Narrows the G.4 `redeem_service` origin from any\\nholder of a valid voucher to the lane's provider. D-0506 item 3 is unchanged.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a75.4 R5.4g, R5.4j; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** This is the design that commits to least. It keeps every check the chain\\ncan enforce (no refund before the deadline, no claim after it, the budget cap, strict voucher\\norder), adds no wire change and no new dependency, and leaves the stronger checkpoint-anchored close\\nopen as a later profile version. The finalized-prefix clock follows \u00a71.2 and \u00a713 F1. Design record,\\nsecond opinions and the implementation handoff are on #1836. Background:\\neconomics, mechanism and security review.\\n\\n## D-0522 \u2014 Agent airdrop spend-to-unlock\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-10-05. The owner's instruction in the session was: fix\\nyellowpaper for \\\"Agent 3:1 spend-to-unlock is ahead of the spec. The WP and the workbook both state it\\nas settled, but the yellowpaper still lists the agent unlock schedule as open (E.38).\\\" This records\\nthat session instruction, not a GitHub review signature. The spend cap (item 2) and the payout and\\nrefund attribution (item 3) are this decision's specification of \\\"3:1\\\" and await the owner's\\nconfirmation in review. Supersedes D-0517 item 4. This documentation changes no runtime behavior; the\\nimplementation is #1175 and #1354.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0517 left the Agent grant's horizon open in E.38, with a linear schedule over\\n`airdrop_vesting_duration_blocks` as the fallback. The tokenomics workbook (Airdrop Schedule \u00a75) and\\nthe draft public whitepaper both publish 3:1 spend-to-unlock with no time vesting: every three locked\\nFLOP spent on compute unlock one, at most three quarters of a grant is spent and one quarter becomes\\nliquid, and principal never spent stays locked. ECON-009 gap G1 objected that 3:1 needs 900,000,000\\nFLOP of locked spend against 823,500,000 of projected demand over years 1\u20133. That objection binds\\nonly a fixed horizon; with no end block, a demand shortfall slows the unlock instead of breaking a\\nschedule.\\n\\nLOCKED_COMPUTE_SPEND returns compute-channel refunds of\\nlocked-funded escrow as ordinary free balance. R8.8 already forbids that (\\\"MUST NOT turn it into\\nfreely transferable credit\\\"), and under spend-to-unlock it would also let an agent open a channel from\\nlocked principal, let it lapse, and recover the whole amount liquid with no compute bought.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. An Agent grant unlocks nothing at its start block and has no end block. It unlocks only against\\n   spend credit: one FLOP of principal for each three FLOP of its locked part in the `payable` of a\\n   session settled by `settle`, `certificate_settle` or `force_settle \u2192 finalize` (the gross\\n   settlement amount before the audit earmark, Appendix F.3), counted once the settlement is\\n   finalized, the channel dispute window has passed, and every fraud dispute raised in that window\\n   has resolved with none upheld. Locked principal spent through `agent_transfer`, refunds, the\\n   R12.1d penalty and any other leg outside the `payable` earn no credit. The unlocked amount is\\n   `\u230aK/3\u230b`, where `K` is the total locked part of credited `payable`s.\\n2. The grant's locked compute spend `C`, net of refunds returned to it, never exceeds `\u230a3P/4\u230b`, where\\n   `P` is the principal at creation. Credit lags spend by finality and the dispute window; without the\\n   cap an agent that spends its whole locked balance first would consume the quarter its credit\\n   unlocks.\\n3. Escrow a channel draws from locked principal, at opening or by top-up, is its locked part. At\\n   close the escrow is allocated once, in order: the `payable` draws on the locked part first; a\\n   refund then draws on what remains of it first and returns that share to the grant as frozen\\n   principal, reducing `C`; every other leg draws on the remainder. A single ordered allocation keeps\\n   each unit of principal counted once. This enforces R8.8's existing rule, and makes the R12.1d\\n   penalty fall on the agent's free funds before its locked principal.\\n4. Principal neither spent nor unlocked stays frozen; its disposition joins the Miner's in E.38.\\n5. `airdrop_vesting_duration_blocks` no longer governs any ratified grant tier. It remains the\\n   airdrop-vesting pallet's interim schedule until R8.7 is implemented.\\n\\n**Worked example.** Across the whole agent pool, `P` = 1,200,000,000: `C` \u2264 900,000,000 and the\\nunlocked total \u2264 300,000,000, the workbook's end state (Airdrop Schedule B90\u2013B91). For one grant of\\n1,200 FLOP: 900 FLOP of locked spend in settled `payable`s unlocks 300 and leaves nothing frozen.\\nCredit counts the agent's spend, not the miner's net receipt, so the 1% audit earmark taken out of\\nthe `payable` does not shave the unlock; counting the miner's 891 instead would unlock 297 and leave\\n3 frozen. If 90 of those 900 went through `agent_transfer` instead, `K` = 810, 270 unlocks, `C` sits at\\nthe cap, and 30 stays frozen under item 4.\\n\\n**Recorded residual.** An agent that controls the miner it pays recovers about 99% of its locked\\nspend as that miner's payout, which the D-0515 compute sinks already allowed, and now also unlocks a\\nthird of it. No runtime common-control rule binds this (E.38, E.49).\\n\\n**Open.** The disposition of principal that never unlocks stays in E.38, and whether a fraud verdict\\nupheld after the dispute window reverses spend credit follows E.44. Whether locked Agent principal may\\nbe staked (the workbook says yes; R8.8 says no) is not changed here.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendix E.38, H.4; the `airdrop_vesting_duration_blocks` and\\n`genesis_agent_airdrop` descriptions.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** The ratio and the cap stay inline in R8.7 until #1175 builds the schedule,\\nas D-0517 did, since a params entry needs a consuming implementation. `agent_transfer` earns no credit\\nbecause it has no settlement record or dispute window to count against. Credit is taken on the\\ngross `payable` because the published rule is \\\"every 3 $FLOP spent on inference releases 1\\\": the\\nagent's spend, of which the audit earmark is part (raised in review of #2381). Counting a payout only once\\nevery timely dispute has resolved, rather than once the window passes with none upheld, keeps a\\nchallenge still pending at the window's end from releasing credit early; a second opinion through\\n`just consult` (Codex) asked for this and for the single ordered allocation of item 3. Workbook: FLOP Tokenomics &\\nAirdrop.xlsx, rev 2026-10-04, Airdrop Schedule \u00a75 (B80 `n` = 3; B89\u2013B91).\\n\\n\\n## D-0523 \u2014 Dormant HTLC timeout burn\\n\\n**Status:** proposal \u2014 pending owner ratification. The shipped burn share stays at zero.\\n\\n**Scope.** The default-zero implementation is prepared alongside receipt metadata and\\nregistered-appraiser redemption. This proposal does not authorize a nonzero runtime setting.\\n\\n**Proposed activation rule.** A separate reviewed runtime upgrade would set a share in\\n0\u20131000 parts per thousand. At a finalized height at or beyond the HTLC deadline, a refund\\nor standard/relay/commit-reveal claim would destroy `floor(reward_amount \u00d7 share / 1000)`\\nof the creator's reserved balance and release the remainder to the refund creator or\\nclaimant respectively. Before that boundary a claim pays the full reward. A refund with\\na positive calculated burn requires the creator's signature; a zero calculated burn\\nretains the existing agent authorization. All burns, payouts and terminal writes are atomic,\\nand insufficient reserved funds reject the resolution. Relayer and commit-reveal settlement\\nremain cross-chain-only and obey the settlement-pause gate.\\n\\n**Economic limit.** Locked reward is not automatically the coalition's extractable value.\\n`HtlcIncentives.collusion_deterred` retains its bribe-bound and honest-inclusion-revenue\\npremises. Honest revenue means unburned fees plus author tips; no value-relative fee floor\\nis enforced. The zero setting establishes no deterrence guarantee, and counter-chain\\ninclusion/finality and pair conformance remain pending E.48.\\n\\n**Before activation.** Ratify the principal-burn policy and outstanding-HTLC treatment,\\nrevalidate the economic mapping and named pair's fee/inclusion budgets, and review the\\nexact runtime upgrade. Zero and armed profiles are covered by pallet tests, economic DRT\\nvectors and the non-vacuous `htlc-timeout-burn.qnt` model; these tests are not activation approval.\\n\\n\\n## D-0524 \u2014 Slot and tranche position rent\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the protocol owner on 2026-10-07 in\\n[task #1965](https://github.com/flop-labs/flop-core/issues/1965). The production activation\\nprofile and numerical tariff, bond and audit parameters remain pending E.54.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** D-0506 item 2's rounding once per provider, and R5.4f's independent\\nprovider cumulative ceilings and replacement entitlement within residual segment budgets.\\nThe escrow ceiling, signed payer cap, checked arithmetic and separate setup/lane holds remain.\\n\\n**Decision.** One rent accumulator per slot and funding tranche, indexed by position in the term.\\nA slot holds `c` encoded bytes. Tranche `k` funds positions `[S_k,S_k+L_k)` at `p_k/q_k`, with\\n`q_k > 0`, for `B_k = ceil(c\u00b7L_k\u00b7p_k/q_k)`. Its accumulator is\\n`R_k(\u03c4) = ceil(c\u00b7(\u03c4\u2212S_k)\u00b7p_k/q_k)`. Accepted interval `[a,b)` earns its historical holder\\n`R_k(b)\u2212R_k(a)`; split an interval at every tranche boundary. Never restart the accumulator\\nat replacement, epoch boundaries or repeat assignment. Never index it by accepted-block count.\\n\\nA replaced provider keeps earned/unreleased rent for its own positions, pending the existing\\nchallenge/forfeiture rule. A replacement receives only its own positions. Failed, unserved and\\nforfeited deltas remain in escrow and refund to the payer; they cannot be reassigned. Close settles\\nsurviving historical entitlements before refunding `\u03a3_k B_k \u2212 paid \u2212 held` per slot; live holds\\ncannot be refunded. Each provider bears its positions' rounding discrepancy: within one tranche,\\nan interval can earn above or below its exact share by strictly less than one base unit. The payer\\nfunds each tranche's ceiling and receives all unearned deltas at close. Fully accepted service\\nreleases every funded ceiling unit and refunds zero. No provider has an independent ceiling guarantee.\\n\\n**Rationale.** Disjoint position deltas telescope within each funded tranche, conserving the cap\\nregardless of release order. Splitting epochs, rotating identities and assigning the same provider\\nagain cannot change payment for the same served positions. New positions use their own funding\\ntranche's tariff. For `c=1,T=2,p/q=1/2`, A earns 1 and B earns 0, rather than the impossible\\nindependent entitlements 1+1 against funding 1. Residual clipping would make earnings depend on\\nrelease order and underpay F in the multi-replacement vector.\\n\\n**Evidence and handoff.** Seven state/balance vectors\\nand `paid-storage-counterexamples.qnt` witnesses supply #1837's payout and #1907's money-path corpus,\\nand #1570/#1571's proof/model coverage. `PaidStorageArithmetic.slot_counter_releases_le_term`\\nproves the term bound; `sl"
